WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.660
Which molecule is made first?

2
00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:08.440
Where does part of a virus end up?

3
00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:10.980
Are the proteins of the body replaced?

4
00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.840
These are questions about the fate of atoms, not about amounts.

5
00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:18.780
The answer is the isotope trick.

6
00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:26.440
Feed a labelled compound A. Later isolate a labelled compound B. Then the conversion

7
00:00:26.440 --> 00:00:28.500
of A to B is established.

8
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:32.760
Pick an isotope of an element the molecule contains.

9
00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.680
It can be radioactive, like carbon-14 or phosphorus-32, or it can be stable and

10
00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:41.280
heavy, like nitrogen-15.

11
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.520
The cell should treat the labelled molecule like the natural one.

12
00:00:46.340 --> 00:00:50.620
Deuterium-labelled metabolites were essentially indistinguishable from natural ones

13
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:53.560
by the methods then used, yet could still be traced.

14
00:00:54.533 --> 00:00:58.373
Feed the labelled compound, or grow cells on it.

15
00:00:58.733 --> 00:01:01.713
Then stop the cells quickly at set times.

16
00:01:02.873 --> 00:01:07.153
Calvin's group fed algae carbon dioxide containing carbon-14.

17
00:01:07.953 --> 00:01:13.073
They took samples after short exposures and dropped them into hot methanol, which

18
00:01:13.073 --> 00:01:14.913
stopped all enzyme reactions.

19
00:01:15.793 --> 00:01:19.913
Then they separated the products on two-dimensional paper chromatography.

20
00:01:20.767 --> 00:01:22.607
Now find the label.

21
00:01:23.647 --> 00:01:25.987
Radioactive decay gives off radiation.

22
00:01:26.947 --> 00:01:32.107
Lay the paper on X-ray film, and each labelled spot darkens the developed film.

23
00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:37.007
Or count the label in a purified fraction with a scintillation counter.

24
00:01:37.887 --> 00:01:40.427
A stable isotope is found by its mass.

25
00:01:41.587 --> 00:01:47.107
Nitrogen-15 was measured by turning a sample's nitrogen into N2 gas and comparing

26
00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:50.627
mass 28 with mass 29 in a mass spectrometer.

27
00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:56.280
A dark spot or a high count means label arrived there.

28
00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:01.920
Its intensity tracks how much label, not how much of the molecule in total.

29
00:02:02.860 --> 00:02:07.040
Here is Shemin, who took nitrogen-15 glycine for three days.

30
00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:13.560
The label in heme rose to a maximum, stayed nearly level for months, then fell.

31
00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:18.460
That gave a red-cell life span of about 127 days.

32
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.890
A label marks anything that takes it up.

33
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:29.510
Hokin and Hokin tried to follow phosphorus-32 into RNA, but the counts kept

34
00:02:29.510 --> 00:02:31.090
vanishing in purification.

35
00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.530
The discarded material was phospholipid.

36
00:02:35.210 --> 00:02:40.050
So identify the labelled compound; do not guess it from where the label shows up.

37
00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:42.990
And half-life is part of the design.

38
00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:47.910
Phosphorus-32 loses half its activity in about 14 days.

39
00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:55.283
Calvin, Benson and Bassham, at Berkeley, fed algae carbon dioxide containing

40
00:02:55.283 --> 00:03:00.703
carbon-14. In a fraction of a second, most of the labelled carbon was in

41
00:03:00.703 --> 00:03:05.463
3-phosphoglyceric acid, the first stable product of carbon dioxide fixation.

42
00:03:06.503 --> 00:03:09.643
A short pulse of label shows which products appear first.

43
00:03:10.983 --> 00:03:16.083
Schoenheimer and Rittenberg, from 1935, found that many body constituents are in

44
00:03:16.083 --> 00:03:21.503
constant renewal. Only a label tells new molecules from old ones of the same kind.

45
00:03:22.317 --> 00:03:28.917
Hershey and Chase, in 1952, tracked the two parts of one virus separately.

46
00:03:29.937 --> 00:03:31.877
Sulfur-35 marks protein.

47
00:03:32.777 --> 00:03:35.837
Phosphorus-32 ends up mostly in DNA.

48
00:03:36.897 --> 00:03:39.677
Most phage DNA entered the bacterium.

49
00:03:40.237 --> 00:03:43.077
Most of the sulfur-containing coat stayed outside.

50
00:03:44.037 --> 00:03:45.997
The cells still made phage.

51
00:03:46.877 --> 00:03:50.237
The authors warned against drawing further chemical inferences.

52
00:03:50.917 --> 00:03:54.837
Keep this rule. A label shows where atoms went.

53
00:03:55.617 --> 00:03:58.257
Identify the compound before you conclude.
